Ran into the same root cause with `gemma4:26b` via Ollama (`@ai-sdk/openai-compatible`). The fix is to explicitly declare `modalities` in `config.json` for the model — this field exists in `provider.ts` but is completely undocumented:

```json
{
  "provider": {
    "ollama": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "options": { "baseURL": "http://localhost:11434/v1" },
      "models": {
        "gemma4:26b": {
          "modalities": {
            "input": ["text", "image"],
            "output": ["text"]
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

Without this, `capabilities.input.image` defaults to `false` for all custom provider models and images are replaced with `"ERROR: Cannot read image (this model does not support image input). Inform the user."` before they ever reach the model.

Allowed values for `modalities.input` / `modalities.output`: `"text"`, `"audio"`, `"image"`, `"video"`, `"pdf"`.

Also worth noting: even with this fixed, MCP tool results containing images still go through the synthetic-user-message detour (not the native tool-result path) because `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` is not in the `supportsMediaInToolResults` allowlist in `message-v2.ts`. Images arrive at the model but as a separate user turn rather than inline in the tool result.
